FX_PSC_deglycoPLAU_3.raw\n6_95_HFX_PSC_BSA_1.raw\n6_95_HFX_PSC_BSA_2.raw\n6_95_HFX_PSC_BSA_3.raw\n6_95_HFX_PSC_PLAU_1.raw\n6_95_HFX_PSC_PLAU_2.raw\n6_95_HFX_PSC_PLAU_3.raw\nHeLa_KP4CMTM3_UV_1.raw\nHeLa_KP4CMTM3_UV_2.raw\nHeLa_KP4CMTM3_UV_3.raw\nHeLa_KP4CMTM3_noUV_1.raw\nHeLa_KP4CMTM3_noUV_2.raw\nHeLa_KP4CMTM3_noUV_3.raw\nHeLa_KP4CMTM9_UV_1.raw\nHeLa_KP4CMTM9_UV_2.raw\nHeLa_KP4CMTM9_UV_3.raw\nHeLa_KP4CMTM9_noUV_1.raw\nHeLa_KP4CMTM9_noUV_2.raw\nHeLa_KP4CMTM9_noUV_3.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nDeciphering intercellular signaling complexes by interaction-guided chemical proteomics\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nIndirect cell-cell interactions mediated by secreted proteins and their plasma membrane receptors play essential roles for regulating intercellular signaling. However, systematic profiling of the interactions between living cell surface receptors and secretome from neighboring cells remains challenging. Here we developed a chemical proteomics approach, termed interaction-guided crosslinking (IGC), to identify ligand-receptor interactions in situ. By introducing glycan-based ligation and click chemistry, the IGC approach via glycan-to-glycan crosslinking successfully captured receptors from as few as 0.1 million living cells using only 10 ng of secreted ligand. The unparalleled sensitivity and selectivity allow systematic crosslinking and identification of ligand-receptor complexes formed between cell secretome and surfaceome in an unbiased and all-to-all manner, leading to the discovery of a novel ligand-receptor interaction between pancreatic cancer cell-secreted urokinase (PLAU) and neuropilin 1 (NRP1) on pancreatic cancer-associated fibroblasts. This approach is thus useful for systematic exploring new ligand-receptor pairs and discovering critical intercellular signaling events.\n\n[METHODS]\nCell Lysis and Pull-Down. After washed with PBS three times, cells were lysed with lysis buffer containing 2% Triton X-100, 150 mM HEPES pH 8.2, 1.5 mM EDTA, 60 mM 2-chloroacetamide, 1 mM phenylmethanesulfonylfluoride, 5 μg/mL aprotinin (Amresco), 5 μg/mL pepstatin (Amresco) and 50 U/mL non-restriction nucleases (Beyotime). The lysate was centrifuged at 14,000g, 4 °C for 6 min, and the resulting supernatant was transferred to a new tube. Streptavidin beads (Thermo Scientific; 1 μL bead volume for 106 cells) were added to the supernatant, and samples were placed on an end-over-end rotator for overnight at 4 °C. Beads were then transferred to spin column and washed three times with washing buffer (6 M urea, 1% SDS, 50 mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.4) and once with 1.5 M NaCl. Then the beads were incubated in alkylation buffer (5 mM TCEP, 50 mM 2-chloroacetamide, 0.2 M ammonium bicarbonate, and 0.5 M NaCl) at 37 °C for 60 min and wash three times with 20% ethanol to completely remove detergents. The purified proteins on beads were digested with 8 ng/μL Trypsin (Promega) and 1.6 ng/μL Lys-C (Wako) in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate at 37 °C overnight on an end-over-end rotator. Digestion mixtures were acidified to pH <3 using trifluoroacetic acid (TFA), desalted with StageTips, and lyophilized. LC-MS/MS Analysis. Peptide samples were dissolved in a solution of 5% ACN and 4% FA. The resulting samples were analyzed by a nano-flow liquid chromatography coupled to a Thermo Fisher Orbitrap Q Exactive, Q Exactive HF-X, Orbitrap Fusion or Bruker timsTOF Pro mass spectrometer. Detailed instrument settings can be found in SI Appendix, Supplemental Methods.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human); Mus musculus (mouse)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
