2026-06-30 15:31:04,823 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-b4615738-e86b-4543-8117-ea08b4a86dbb', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to sample preparation and digestion.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: cleavage agent details\nsdrf_column: comment[cleavage agent details]\nontology: MS:1001044 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Enzyme or chemical used for protein digestion.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology terms. Multiple enzymes are common in XL-MS.\n  If multiple, return a list.\n  Common terms:\n    Trypsin (MS:1001251) — cleaves K,R (not before P)\n    Lys-C (MS:1001309) — cleaves K\n    Lys-C/P (MS:1001309) — cleaves K including before P\n    Asp-N (MS:1001303) — cleaves N-terminal to D\n    Glu-C (MS:1001917) — cleaves C-terminal to E, D\n    Chymotrypsin (MS:1001306) — cleaves F,Y,W,L\n    No cleavage (MS:1001955)\n    Unspecific cleavage (MS:1001956)\n  Format each as: NT=<enzyme name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: label\nsdrf_column: comment[label]\nontology: PRIDE:0000514, MS\ndescription: Labeling strategy used for quantification.\ninstructions: Most XL-MS is label-free. Use:\n  NT=label free sample;AC=MS:1002038 — for label-free\n  NT=SILAC;AC=MS:1002038 — for SILAC\n  NT=iTRAQ4plex;AC=PRIDE:0000114 — for iTRAQ 4-plex\n  NT=TMT6plex;AC=PRIDE:0000116 — for TMT\n  If not stated, assume label free.\n\nterm: alkylation reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[alkylation reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000598\ndescription: Chemical reagent used for cysteine alkylation (typically before digestion).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Common: iodoacetamide (IAA), iodoacetic acid,\n  N-ethylmaleimide (NEM), acrylamide. If iodoacetamide, use "iodoacetamide".\n\nterm: fractionation method\nsdrf_column: comment[fractionation method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000550\ndescription: Peptide fractionation method used before LC-MS/MS (distinct from crosslink\n  enrichment — this is standard proteomics fractionation).\ninstructions: Return null if no pre-LC fractionation. Examples: "high-pH reversed-phase\n  chromatography", "basic reversed-phase LC", "SCX fractionation", "HILIC".\n\nterm: modification parameters\nsdrf_column: comment[modification parameters]\nontology: MS:1001055, Unimod, PSI-MOD\ndescription: Post-translational modifications searched in the database search.\noutput_format: List of modifications, each formatted as:\n  NT=<mod name>;AC=<Unimod accession>;TA=<target amino acid>;MT=<Fixed or Variable>\ninstructions: Always include carbamidomethyl cysteine if IAA alkylation was used:\n  NT=Carbamidomethyl;AC=UNIMOD:4;TA=C;MT=Fixed\n  Always include oxidation of methionine if searched:\n  NT=Oxidation;AC=UNIMOD:21;TA=M;MT=Variable\n  Include crosslinker mono-link (dead-end) modifications if mentioned.\n  Return a list even if only one modification.\n\nterm: fraction identifier\nsdrf_column: comment[fraction identifier]\nontology: MS:1000858\ndescription: Fraction number for fractionated samples.\ninstructions: Describe whether the dataset uses fractionation and what the fractionation\n  scheme is (e.g. "12 SEC fractions per replicate", "no fractionation").\n  The actual integer values are assigned per-file; describe the scheme here.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nFor comment[modification parameters], "value" should be a list of formatted strings.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nDeciphering intercellular signaling complexes by interaction-guided chemical proteomics\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nIndirect cell-cell interactions mediated by secreted proteins and their plasma membrane receptors play essential roles for regulating intercellular signaling. However, systematic profiling of the interactions between living cell surface receptors and secretome from neighboring cells remains challenging. Here we developed a chemical proteomics approach, termed interaction-guided crosslinking (IGC), to identify ligand-receptor interactions in situ. By introducing glycan-based ligation and click chemistry, the IGC approach via glycan-to-glycan crosslinking successfully captured receptors from as few as 0.1 million living cells using only 10 ng of secreted ligand. The unparalleled sensitivity and selectivity allow systematic crosslinking and identification of ligand-receptor complexes formed between cell secretome and surfaceome in an unbiased and all-to-all manner, leading to the discovery of a novel ligand-receptor interaction between pancreatic cancer cell-secreted urokinase (PLAU) and neuropilin 1 (NRP1) on pancreatic cancer-associated fibroblasts. This approach is thus useful for systematic exploring new ligand-receptor pairs and discovering critical intercellular signaling events.\n\n[METHODS]\nCell Lysis and Pull-Down. After washed with PBS three times, cells were lysed with lysis buffer containing 2% Triton X-100, 150 mM HEPES pH 8.2, 1.5 mM EDTA, 60 mM 2-chloroacetamide, 1 mM phenylmethanesulfonylfluoride, 5 μg/mL aprotinin (Amresco), 5 μg/mL pepstatin (Amresco) and 50 U/mL non-restriction nucleases (Beyotime). The lysate was centrifuged at 14,000g, 4 °C for 6 min, and the resulting supernatant was transferred to a new tube. Streptavidin beads (Thermo Scientific; 1 μL bead volume for 106 cells) were added to the supernatant, and samples were placed on an end-over-end rotator for overnight at 4 °C. Beads were then transferred to spin column and washed three times with washing buffer (6 M urea, 1% SDS, 50 mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.4) and once with 1.5 M NaCl. Then the beads were incubated in alkylation buffer (5 mM TCEP, 50 mM 2-chloroacetamide, 0.2 M ammonium bicarbonate, and 0.5 M NaCl) at 37 °C for 60 min and wash three times with 20% ethanol to completely remove detergents. The purified proteins on beads were digested with 8 ng/μL Trypsin (Promega) and 1.6 ng/μL Lys-C (Wako) in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate at 37 °C overnight on an end-over-end rotator. Digestion mixtures were acidified to pH <3 using trifluoroacetic acid (TFA), desalted with StageTips, and lyophilized. LC-MS/MS Analysis. Peptide samples were dissolved in a solution of 5% ACN and 4% FA. The resulting samples were analyzed by a nano-flow liquid chromatography coupled to a Thermo Fisher Orbitrap Q Exactive, Q Exactive HF-X, Orbitrap Fusion or Bruker timsTOF Pro mass spectrometer. Detailed instrument settings can be found in SI Appendix, Supplemental Methods.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
